    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="w(x) = (x - 1)(x - 2) \cdots (x - 20) has well separated integer roots, yet James Wilkinson found that subtracting 2^{-23} from its x^{19} coefficient, -210, sends ten of the roots far into the complex plane. The root condition numbers explain why: the roots near 16 amplify a relative change in that coefficient by a factor of about 3 \times 10^{10}.">

